# CODEOWNERS — review requirements for the paths that matter to brand
# integrity: the trust root (canonical logos + the manifest that hashes
# them) and the automation that is able to rewrite that trust root.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ASSUMING THIS FILE ENFORCES ANYTHING:
#
# GitHub only turns a CODEOWNERS entry into an actual merge requirement when
# the target branch's branch protection rule has "Require review from Code
# Owners" enabled. As measured for this repo, branch protection currently
# has NO required reviews and enforce_admins is false — that setting has
# NOT been turned on. Enabling it is the Director's call (a repo-settings
# change), not something this file can do or imply on its own.
#
# Until that setting is enabled, this file is ADVISORY ONLY. It documents
# who *should* review these paths, and GitHub will still show them as a
# suggested reviewer on a PR touching these globs — but nothing blocks a
# merge without that review, including a direct push by anyone with write
# access. Do not describe this file as an enforced control in any doc,
# summary, or scorecard. See .github/SECURITY-CONTROLS.md for the full,
# honest gap list and what closing it would actually require.
#
# Owner: @mcp-tool-shop — the personal account used for review on this
# mcp-tool-shop-org repo (see .claude/rules/canonical-ownership.md: tool
# repos are owned under the org, reviewed by the personal account).
#
# Later entries override earlier ones for the same path (standard
# CODEOWNERS precedence) — there is no catch-all `*` entry here on purpose;
# scope is intentionally limited to the paths that matter, not every file.

# --- The trust root ---------------------------------------------------------
# Exactly the surface a swapped-logo attack touches: the canonical image
# files, and the SHA-256 manifest that is supposed to attest to them.
# Reminder (see .github/SECURITY-CONTROLS.md): the manifest alone does not
# stop someone who can also regenerate it — review is the actual control.
/logos/**       @mcp-tool-shop
/manifest.json  @mcp-tool-shop

# --- The automation that can rewrite the trust root above -------------------
# A compromise here can regenerate a passing manifest around a tampered
# logo just as easily as editing /logos/** directly — CI, the sync
# tripwire, and the manifest/verify pipeline all live under these paths.
/.github/**  @mcp-tool-shop
/scripts/**  @mcp-tool-shop
